the EWS software that would support the VAXstation II, VAXstation II/GPX, VAXstation 3500, VAXstation 2000, 
and all VAXstation 3100 systems as X terminals (the Model 76 came later, so it wasn't supported)

The first version of the EWS software had some limitations (bugs?) but has evolved and has been replaced with
the VXT software (which doesn't support all of those platforms unfortunately)   


RE: using Model 76 as a cluster node, etc.

Yes, you can do that.  In fact, there are a couple of files that you can edit into your SYS$SPECIFIC  root on the
Model 76 to allow remote session managers to start up.  (the DCL to do the session startup is pretty simple, in
fact, you could probably use the stuff from the EWS terminal startup to make it work)

DECW$SERVER_ACCESS_TRUSTED.DAT should contain the names of all hosts that can change the security
characteristics of the server.  You'll have to put your Alpha host in there in order to start the session manager.  
The file is documented (I think, it's been a while ) with a template file.

DECW$SERVER_ACCESS_ALLOWED.DAT should contain the names of all hosts that are allowed access to the
server, but cannot change the security.  


When you boot the VAXstation, it executes its private copy of DECW$STARTUP.com that doesn't start the 
session manager, but then goes off to the host to start the session on the remote node.  It can all be done 
pretty automatically, and I've seen several people here at Digital do it to make use of Model 76 systms 
hanging off of Alpha systems.   (making it work under OSF takes a little more work , but it can be done)




