From the CMD CQD 220 manual, pp24:

	__________________
        |  9  7  5  3  1 |
        | 10  8  6  4  2 |
        ------------------

Pin 1:  Drive 1 on line, input/output signal, normally high.
    2:  Ground.
    3:  TXD for RS232 application, transmit data.
    4:  Ground.
    5:  Drive 0 access output signal, active low.
    6:  Drive 1 access output signal, active low.
    7:  Drive 1 write protect, input signal, active low.
    8:  Drive 0 write protect, input signal, active low.
        or for RS232 application, receive data.
    9:  Ground.
   10:  Drive 0 on line, input/output signal, normally high

Note that for CMD's on board RS232 utility, only pin 3, 8 and grounds are 
used.

(DEC makes a cable that is perfect for this - I think it is the console cable 
 for old LSI-11 machines - I can dig up the part # if you want)


I had experienced the same problems you describe on a 220 - turned out to be 
a flakey Conner drive - they are not my first choice of drives in a multi-
disk systems for a lot of reasons.

