In addition to Terry Kennedy's excellent advice, you might check that the
controller is at the proper address. You can do this from the console
emulator, which you start by

1) Putting the HALT ENABLE toggle switch on dotted circle.
2) Pressing RESET on the front panel.

When you have the console prompt, >>>, you can check the controller
register by

   >>>E/P/W 20001468

If you get a value, the controller is responding at the correct address for
the BOOT code.  If it's at an incorrect, or no, address, you'll get an
ILL ADR error.

You can also attempt boots manually from the console prompt, by

   >>>B DUcu   where c=controller (A,B,...) and u=unit (0,1,...)

The automatic boot is supposed to try all controller and unit combinations
for you.
