We added 5 2.1gb Seagate drives last summer, and learned a few lessons:
 
1. Stay away from single drive enclosures. You will quickly exhaust your SCSI
chain length limit.  There are several third party 3.5" stackables out there
that allow up to 6 devices.  Check out APS.
 
2. If you do go with single or dual drive enclosures, beware of cheap SCSI
cables and passive terminators.  Look for fully shielded (all the way to the
metal band on the cable head) and twisted pair cable.  Throw away the crap
thats about 3/8" in diameter.  I don't want to sound like an APS shill, but
they do sell good cables, and Digital Active Terminators that do help eliminate
SCSI Bus noise and errors.
 
3. If possible, obtain disktab entries from the manufacturer.  If you are at
Ultrix 4.2a or beyond, I believe that newfs can obtain the geometry from the
disk itself by using:
 
    /etc/newfs /dev/rrz#c /dev/rrz#c
 
On our Seagates, I obtained the geometry from SEAGATEs EXCELLENT FAXback server,
and guessed at my own newfs parameters.
 
4. If you are going to put these drives on DEC maintanence, talk to your field
rep to make sure that 1) they ar supported and 2) the local office can place
their hands on one within 2 hours.  We've taken all of our SCSI drives off
DEC maintanence and purchased some spares.  The new drives that we purchase
have a 5 year return to factory warantee, so if they break, we crack open the
spare and send the old one back to the factory (haven't had to yet, knock..).
It just didn't make sense to keep them on maintanence when we could self-
maintain much cheaper.
 
5. Don't forget to budget for additional backup tapes, and maybe even an
additional tape drive.  Also, don't underestimate the additional time of
a full set of level 0 dumps.  I now spend every first Saturday babysitting
level0's and VMS Standalones because we are a little short on high
capacity tape drives.
 
All in all, I'd let price/storage/warantee period be my guide.  We just got
a refurbed VS4000-60 with 2 DEC DSP5200 drives, and they are very nice indeed.
I've heard that the RZ26L (actually a Seagate) is quite noisy and should
be avoided unless you work in a noisy factory.
 
Good luck.
 
