I haven't seen much mention of the CMD Q-bus SCSI controllers on this list,
but hope somebody out there has some experience with them and can help me.

My system is an old VAXstation II running Ultrix 4.0.  It has a VCB-01
monochrome graphics controller, TK50 tape, DHV-11 serial comms, a DEQNA,
and an RQDX3 controller configured as a second disk controller.
There are 4 SCSI devices running off a CMD CQD-220/M SCSI controller,
0/boot:	Quantum LPS240S		(240 Mb)
1/swap:	Connor CP3040		(40 Mb)
2:	Seagate Wren VII	(1 Gb)
4:	Ricoh RS9200E MO	(~330 Mb per MO disk side)

This system has given sterling service for years, and has had bits added
to it over time but on the whole has been very stable with the above
configuration.  In the last four weeks, however, it has developed an
irritating intermittent problem whereby it either crashes (without
rebooting) or simply hangs, accepting TCP/IP connections but not doing
anything.  I've noticed the occasional "controller error" message appearing
in the error log (see appended) with some hung states, but not all hangs
and crashes get logged.

Hitting reset after a hang will sometimes get the system running happily
again (with no dump) but sometimes the CMD controller seems to have gone
south and will not be found by the microVAX.  Other times, the controller
is found but it doesn't find any disks on the SCSI chain.

Figuring out where the problem may lie is made difficult by the lack of
more information in the error log, by the variable nature of the symptoms,
and by not easily being able to run the on-board CQD-220 diagnostics
(with the VCB01 graphics controller I can't use the on-board utility, and
CMD never responded to my fax requests for the pinouts on the diagnostic
on-board serial port).  I'm pretty sure it's not the SCSI chain, as
this problem happens even with a single temporary disk connected with 10 cm
of SCSI cabling straight to the controller.

Has anyone come across a similarly erratic CMD controller, or am I missing
some obvious way of getting more information about the crashes?

With thanks in anticipation,
Alex.
