> more seasoned VMS systems geek^H^H^H^Hmanager.

Yes, it's not really intended for first-day-on-the-job system managers.  It
doesn't talk about actual system management at all.  It is an introductory
internals book that teaches advanced system managers and budding system
programmers how VMS works on the inside.  For instance, if a system manager
understands the internals of virtual memory, it's easier to comprehend how
various sysgen parameters can be changed to tune the system.

I wasn't going to mention my book, because I didn't think it applied to the
original post, but if anybody is interested, it is: Inside VMS, The System
Manager's and System Programmer's Guide to VMS Internals, Van Nostrand
Reinhold, ISBN 0-442-00474-5.
Also, VNR has another book that is probably closer to what the poster
wanted:
Introductory Guide to VMS System Management, Joesph Stith, ISBN
0-442-00403-6.

Both books are part of the VNR DEC series, edited by Terry Shannon.
