References: <willmore.771625333@tremplo.gis.iastate.edu>
NNTP-Posting-Host: hafnium.cchem.berkeley.edu
Keywords: RD53

Try removing the copper strip with a graphite tablet on it, which is
in the center of the electronics board. It may remedy the problem for
a few months to a year. However, the drive is essntially doomed anyway.
If the said part is already removed, start looking for another drive...
I tried to repair these non-spinning drives to no avail. My current
perception of this problem is that the bearings get worn out, so the 
friction increases, which causes the current drawn by the motor to increase.
When it increases high enough, it trips the protection circuit in the
motor power board (the smaller board in the drive). Sometimes changing
this board helps, but not for a long time. In fact, I haven't yet seen
a Micropolis 1325 which ended its life in a way different from refusing
to spin up.

