In article <Cr9BB6.GCJ@world.std.com>, RAID7@world.std.com (John OBrien) writes:
>>double your hardware, or triple if you can afford it.
> 
> CPU upgrades generally are not needed now when there is a problem with 
> RDBMS performance.  What systems administrators need to do to preserve 
> their investment in their existing CPUs is just put in RAID I/O 
> acceleration technology to lift the I/O speed well up above that of the 
> single-spindle disk I/O rate.  Typically I/O throughput increases of 2X 
> and 3X are obtained with UNIX systems, and I/O throughput increases of 
> 10X are not uncommon with the VAX.  I have written extensively on this in 
> the computer press and would be happy to share the material with 
> technical personnel.

The above is of course only true if your system is I/O bound.  The whole
process of tuning a database is to balance the queueing on all system
resources.

This can be demonstrated on any system where you have either a cpu bottleneck
ora an I/O bottleneck.  In the case of a cpu bottleneck, you simply reduce
the number of buffers in the SGA to something real small and WOW your cpu is
hardly utilised now BUT your disk is REALLY active.  

When we moved from Oracle V6 to Oracle7 I had this cunning plan and linked the
SGA much larger and doubled the number of buffers.  The machine immediately
became CPU bound with massive queues (very little I/O queueing though).  We
had to drop the buffers back to the Oracle V6 settings.  Actually we had to go
a little less as we were running the Oracle7 database on a faster disk so more
load was being transferred from the disk queues to the cpu queues.

Nice try ... but try to keep the advertising to a minimum please 8^}.

I would like to see your extensive writings and how they justify your 
statement above "What systems administrators need to do to preserve their
investment in their existing CPUs is just put in RAID I/O ...". 

There is no black and white in database tuning.  It's always a matter of
balance.  Give me supercomputer (with slow or fast disks) and I can create 
an SGA large enough to cause cpu bottlenecks.

