veen@nl101.ittpub.nl (Hans van Veen - ITT Gouden Gids) writes:

>We are currently investigating the possibilities of replacing our RA90 based
>storage environment.

>Environment: VAX 6510, VAX6520, 4*HSC70, 50 Gbyte (mostly RA90) storage.

>We have had an quote of a local company, offering the CMD HAWK storage server,
>and the RAID-7 storage system of Storage Computer.

>CMD is rather well known, but Storage Computer not. Has anyone experiences
>with this comapany. Is it 'solid' (CMD and EuroLogic who distributes CMD stuff
>in Europe are).

>Also has anyone any experiences with RAID-7 from Storage Computer ??

We have been running the StorComp RAID-7 product for over a year now on
several systems (DECsystem and VAX) and not without problems.  StorComp
uses me for a lot of their DEC references; many of their customers
will not act as references for competitive reasons.  I do not have a 
100% glowing report; however, we continue to use their product and feel 
that we have made a wise choice.

Our environment is as follows:  there are three systems connected to 
the unit; a DECsystem 5500 running Ultrix 4.2, a VAX 4000-300 running
VMS 5.5, and an AXP 3000-500 running OpenVMS.  The 5500 runs a database
application (uniVerse), the 4000-300 runs another database application
(Ingres), and the AXP runs Pathworks.  We are able to share RAID-7
files between the AXP and the VAX using VMS clustering successfully. 

So here are the facts:  

1 hardware failure (cache board)
4 software failures (1 while the unit was in our own internal testing)

StorComp has been great about resolving software problems.  They
have not delayed in assisting in the resolution of bugs/glitches we
have had.

We have not lost data due to the failures; they have, however, caused
downtime to ensure the integrity of our data and allow us to conduct 
problem resolution.

Performance:

As for the 5500, performance is difficult to tell as we are limited by our
3.3Mbyte Qbus.  This will soon change, as this system will be upgraded
to an AXP 3000-800S.  We have seen upwards of 1700 bps on a 1 second 
update, and up to 800 bps on 5 second update of iostat.  On the 
4000-300 VAX system, we see 100% CPU utilization while doing 
database queries, so it is safe to say that the RAID-7 system is 
maxing it out.  As for the AXP system (Pathworks server), users 
report faster file access than local IDE hard drives.  We have seen 
upwards of 150 QIOs/second peak on this system.

We have had excellent luck with the CMD controllers (CQD443 Qbus
to SCSI-II [2 each] and a CTC3000 TurboChannel to SCSI-II).  I was
very disapointed to find out that the CTC3000 will not work under
OSF and I am relegated to using the DEC.  This is from a reseller - I
am going to double check with CMD.

"Your milage may vary."  Your experiences may not reflect ours.  I
encourage you to at least give it a look-see.

Bob Haskins
haskins@mipps.myapc.com


