The DECserver 2100 is a 'tightly coupled multiprocessor'.  All processors
have access to all of physical memory, and the access time is the same 
for all processors.

DEC OSF/1 3.0 supports SMP (symetrical multiprocessesing).  A single
copy of OSF1 is being executed by the multiple processors.  Software
locks (which are built using CPU instructions designed for the purpose)
protect the important data structures such as the queue of disk
requests.  If a thread running on one processor wants to perform disk
i/o it must obtain the proper lock that will allow it to queue the
request.  The same method is used to allow sharing of all system
services by threads running on the processors.

The DECserver 2100 appears to external systems to be one system.  It
really is just one system because there is only one copy of OSF/1
running on the 2100.

This one system can export its file systems via NFS, it can import file
systems from other hosts via NFS, and it can use ASE to provide
failover protection for these file systems.  It can also use LSM to
stripe, mirror or concatenate seperate disk partitions.

