> CPU upgrades generally are not needed now when there is a problem with 
> RDBMS performance.  What systems administrators need to do to preserve 
> their investment in their existing CPUs is just put in RAID I/O 
> acceleration technology to lift the I/O speed well up above that of the 
> single-spindle disk I/O rate.  

A lot of that may have to do with the particular RAID box and RDBMS 
you're looking at. With a sybase packet size of 2K, not all RAID boxes
will accelerate that (some may even degrade performance further). 
Interestingly, on some HW platforms, I can get a fully mirrored multi-
spindle disk configuration for close to the same money as an equally 
sized RAID configuration. With a little creative placement, I can 
distribute my I/O's and get some of the same performance boosts (possibly
even better). Of course, that depends a lot on the i/o profile 
of the app. RAID will give you a fairly consistent boost, whereas
distribution of i/o over smaller, regular drives is not guranteed
and requires some fairly detailed knowledge of both the app and the
business. In general, RAID (if it works with your packet size)
is a safe bet; however, if you have the time and the knowledge, 
distributing over multiple spindles outside a RAID configuration
may be a better choice. It depends on your hardware, your application,
the amount of knowledge in-house and how much money you're willing
to spend.

I agree that upgrading a CPU is not nescessary unless you know that
a) you're CPU bound, and b) the CPU bound processes are not i/o
related (i.e. the i/o bound processes is chewing up CPU time to
negotiate it's i/o). 

On the same concept of RAID is building distributed apps to run on
inexpensive platforms. If the app is constructed to take advantage 
of load balancing accross n platforms, you can size (and more
importantly, re-size) hardware quickly, cleanly, cheaply and 
easily. The overhead involved in building apps distributed over 
n platforms is prohibitive for the small installation; but for
the large installation, multiple installations of varying sizes,
or installations where size can change rapidly and unpredictably, 
then it's a good direction to go.


