In article <BEACOM.94Jun8120157@phenoo.physics.wisc.edu>, beacom@phenoo.physics.wisc.edu (John Beacom) writes:
|> 
|> i have read (in _essential system administration_, by aeleen frisch),
|> that tar archives written to tape will choke and die completely if 
|> there is a bad spot in the tape, whereas with a cpio archive one would
|> be able to at least recover the rest of the data.
|> 
|> i would like to know how robust dump archives are in this respect.  any
|> ideas?  

I, for one, never worry about the tape.  Whenever I /need/, /really need/ to
restore stuff from tape I find 
	a) some machine in the loop crashed that night, so the dump didn't 
	get done
	b) the wrong tape was loaded and
		i) the data I wanted was overwriten, or
		ii) the tape got to the end prematurely and couldn't
		complete.
	c) the dump operator was out sick that day and didn't load the
	tape at all
	d) I made a typo in the dump command files which meant that the
	backups I thought were happening weren't (I found the problem the
	next day, but of course that was the night I needed it).
	e) Need I go on?
I have /often/ wanted to restore files from tape and couldn't, but I have
/never/ had it happen because of a bad physical tape.  

Over the years I have developed a number of schemes to guarantee (!) the 
availability of backup info.  Almost all of them have to do with backstopping
against human error or absence.

