 would like some comments/advice.  Here is my situation, I will be starting as a
professor this fall and I have start-up funds of about $10,000-$15,000 to
put towards a work station.  My primary use of the work station will be
Monte Carlo simulations.  Therefore, number crunching throughput is the
most important criterion.  The programs for the simulations are self
written in FORTRAN, so that compiler will be necessary.  I would like to
have some visualization capabilities, to view the final data and for
presentations, but alot of buzzes and whistles are not important.  I am
considering the following computers:   DEC alphas, SUN SPARC 20, or a
Silicon Graphics in the Indigo family, something like the Indigo 2XL.  

With that in mind, here are my questions/considerations.  

  1.)  What are the operating systems like, i.e. how difficult is system
administration?  I plan to administer it myself.  Also what is the company
support like?  Are they quick at responding to problems?  Or are you on
hold for hours trying to get through?

  2.)  What I'm looking for is the best bang for my buck.  Is a DEC alpha
worth the (assumed) increased price?  Can SUN or SG give me quality
throughput?  Does anyone have timing comparisons from the three dealers?  I
realize timing is application dependent, but I need to start somewhere.

  3.)  Does anyone know of the ability to connect to any of these machines to
time my program on it?

  4.)  Is any one of these computers more or less expandable than the others?

  5.)  If you were in the same boat, which computer would you buy, and why?  

