In article <2t15r2$29f@herald.indirect.com>, chainsaw@indirect.com (Chainsaw) writes:

>	Recently I got a DECstation 3100 with the RZ23 Hard drive in it.  
>I have little to no previous experience with DEC's and was wondering what 
>I need to make the DEC boot on the Drive.  The drive does not spin up 
>when I start the unit, this may be a bad drive or just something I have 
>to do.  I have taken the drive and put it on a PC with an adaptec 1542CF 
>controller and it has recognized the drive, but also did not spin it 
>up.   ANy help/Info would be greatly appreciated.  I am very new to this 
>unit and am looking for any info/manuals available.

The boot code in the DECstation 3100 sends the drive an explicit
command to spin up.  This allows the spin up of the drives in the
system to be staggered, reducing the load on the power supply.
Most DEC disks are configured to wait for the command, rather
than spin up on power on.  Depending on the drive the behaviour
can be changed by a jumper or mode page change in the drive.

The rzdisk utility on ULTRIX will let you make the firmware change.

The RZ23 is only big enough for a root file system and page/swap
space, so it can't have a whole system disk on it.  Unless you
know that it is bootable I think it is unlikely that has a minimal
bootable system on it.  Still, it doesn't hurt to try:

	>>boot -f rz(0,#,0)vmunix

Replace the '#' with the target number of the disk.  This will
try to boot from that disk.

"Diskfull" DECstations were usually sold with an RZ55, RZ56 or
RZ57 (5.25" form factor drives) for the system disk.  When the
RZ23 was included it was typically used for additional page
and swap space and small file systems.  Many (otherwise) diskless
systems were sold with one of the internal drives to provide
local page/swap space.

