OK, I checked it out. The SCSI of the 2000 is only intended for the SCSI
variant of the TK50. No support for disks is available. The hacked driver job
is a. VMS only b. _definitely_ DEC internal since it is based on the original
VMS source code.

The discussions in our internal VAX Notes system indicate that getting disks
to work on the 2000 was full of pitfalls. The only drive tested was the RRD40
cdrom drive, and that had lots of quirks. The hardware is based on the shaky
SCSI spec of the day the uvax 2000 was devised, which is not much help.

So: you are all stuck with the 2x RD54 on the uVAX2000, giving you something like
280Mb disk space. Or find a friendly VAX cluster and use their disks ;-)

Wilko

