In article <1994Jun7.134233.4642@news.datasrv.co.il>, ncc@zeus.datasrv.co.il writes:

> It seems like 'cvd' is trying to lookup a null-named font! Can this be
> right? Could it be that 'cvd' is relying on a non-standard feature?

No, cvd certainly doesn't rely on looking up a null-named font.  It
may be that we're trying to use a font that isn't available to your
server.

The fonts used by CASEVision tools are specified in the scheme files.
You can look up the font names there, and use xlsfonts to see whether
your server has access to all of them.  You didn't specify what
version of WorkShop you're using, so it's a little tricky for me to
tell you what file to look in: depending on your OS+WorkShop version,
you'll find either a directory named /usr/lib/X11/Schmes, or one named
/usr/lib/X11/schemes (either upper or lower case "s" in the word
"schemes").  In WorkShop 1.* (for Irix 4.*), the schemes are part of
the CaseVision image (and the directory is named "Schemes"); in later
WorkShop/Irix combinations, they're part of the the OS itself (and the
directory is renamed "schemes," in order to allow both scheme systems
to be installed on the same system, for running old-scheme-using
programs on new-scheme systems).

In this schemes directory, you'll find subdirectories whose names are
the names of the available schemes (a scheme sets colors, fonts, and
other resources in a coordinated group).  Now, we need to know what
scheme you're using.  Since you didn't know about schemes, you
probably haven't changed your scheme (you're using the default, shades
of grey with pink type-in areas, right?).  Under the "schemes"
directory, this scheme is called "Base"; under the "Schemes"
directory, it's called Lascaux.

Now, find the file named "FontPalette", or if there isn't one, find
the file named "palette" (again, a version variation).  You'll find
that either file is devided into "use small fonts" area and its
complement.  Which one gets picked is a function of your display
hardware; the easiest thing at this point would be to ignore this, and
pull out the list of all fonts mentioned in the entire file.  Feed
these to xlsfonts, one by one; I suspect you'll find that at least one
of them is unknown to your X server.  Either provide that font for
your server, or change the palette file to name a font that is
available.

But I don't see how any of that would make your cursor disappear; I
think you must have two problems.


