In article <2sgbah$3m5@news.cerf.net>, magma@nic.cerf.net (Ed Romascan) writes:
>   SUN has used the Z8530 SCC on their workstations, I'm not
> sure about DEC.

Yes, DEC uses the Z8530, at least on the 3000/400 and 3000/500. On those 
machines, it is connected to a small DMA engine that can receive up to
1024 bytes (although it is usually configured to receive 513 bytes; it gives
an interrupt when it crosses the halfway point, you see).
> 
>   I hear someone asking, "at what line speeds?".  DECstation 5000/200
> starts puking at as low as 19200.  More dismaying is that a flamingo
> (3000/500 AXP) will begin to overrun at 38400.

Actually if you have are running OSF/1 V1.2 or 1.3 and have enough memory,
it can puke at 300 baud even with an 8 character FIFO in the UART. Fixed
in 2.0, though...

The major difficulty with Unix is that you don't have very good control over
interrupt latencies in device drivers. The major problems that I've had with
the DECstations losing characters involve doing things on the graphics console
while data is flowing in at high baud rates.
