In article <dave.19.000FF643@bcl.co.nz> dave@bcl.co.nz (Dave Green) writes:
>
>I have a DLT2000 tape drive that exhibits very slow data transfer rates on 
>both Alpha and Sun systems.
>
>On the Alpha/OpenVMS system, backup aborts with "too many errors" message 
>while on a Sparc 2 system the drive seems to do a lot of tape positioning and 
>a dump command gives an esimated transfer time of 12 hours for a 600Mb 
>transfer.

Can't help you with the Suns, but my experience with a DEC TZ87
(non-oem version of the DLT2000) has been excellent.  These drives are
capable of transfer rates of a little over 1 MB/sec for non-compressed
mode, and up to 2 MB/sec for compressed mode (depending on how
compressible your data is).

If the software driver fails to turn on the "buffered" bit when
initializing the tape device, then you might observe the symptoms you
describe.  If that bit is not turned on, the drive won't stream, and
you'll see transfer rates down around 15 KB/sec with repositioning
between each record.  Did you follow the directions for configuring
the drive with your Sun system?

As for the VMS problems, maybe there really is a hardware problem.
Have you looked at your VMS error log?
-- 
